@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Variant isolating the lollipop-and-socket mechanism on a
'                 single pair of components. Contrasts (1) a provided
'                 interface (ball), (2) a required interface (socket), and
'                 (3) an assembly connector (ball plugged into socket).
'   usecase:      Teach the three primitive interface relationships in UML
'                 component diagrams with minimal surrounding noise.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       UML component-diagram mechanics
' ============================================================================
title Example Component Diagram - Provided vs Required Interfaces

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' STYLE: keep it stark so the shapes are the only thing on screen.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
skinparam componentStyle uml2
skinparam shadowing false

' Two reusable components. We will show three different relationships
' against the SAME pair so the reader can compare side by side.
component "Component A (Provider)" as a
component "Component B (Consumer)" as b

' ===========================================================================
' (1) PROVIDED INTERFACE ONLY — the ball / lollipop.
'     Component A offers a contract; nothing is wired to it yet.
'     Notation: [A] -()- IName   (ball sticks out of A)
' ===========================================================================
() "Provided Only (ball)" as iProvided
a -()- iProvided
' Nothing attaches to iProvided -> it dangles as an offer.

' ===========================================================================
' (2) REQUIRED INTERFACE ONLY — the socket / cup.
'     Component B needs a contract; no provider is shown yet.
'     Notation: [B] ..() IName   (cup opens out of B)
' ===========================================================================
() "Required Only (socket)" as iRequired
b ..() iRequired
' Nothing satisfies iRequired -> it dangles as a need.

' ===========================================================================
' (3) ASSEMBLY CONNECTOR — the ball plugs INTO the socket.
'     One side provides (-()-), the other requires (..()). PlantUML joins
'     them on the shared interface node, forming the classic lollipop-in-cup.
'     This is how two components actually connect at runtime.
' ===========================================================================
() "Assembly (ball into socket)" as iAssembly
a -()- iAssembly     ' A provides the contract (ball)
b ..() iAssembly     ' B requires that same contract (cup) -> they connect

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' SIDE-BY-SIDE LEGEND pinned under the diagram body.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
note bottom of b
  **(1) Provided** iProvided   — ball on A; offered, not yet consumed.
  **(2) Required** iRequired   — cup on B; needed, not yet satisfied.
  **(3) Assembly** iAssembly   — A's ball plugs into B's cup = a live link.
  ----
  Rule: an assembly connector is just a provided and a required interface
  that reference the SAME contract, drawn so the shapes mate.
end note

@enduml
